- Just wait a minute, one more--

00:12:50.830 --> 00:12:52.460
- --just to hone your vision.

00:12:52.460 --> 00:12:53.320
--then I'll be gone.

00:12:53.320 --> 00:12:54.470
No I won't.

00:12:54.470 --> 00:12:56.470
- If you want to be a
photographer, particularly

00:12:56.470 --> 00:12:59.110
a photojournalist, you want
to learn about the world,

00:12:59.110 --> 00:13:01.240
and you want to
learn about yourself.

00:13:01.240 --> 00:13:05.000
And you want to find things
that you genuinely care about,

00:13:05.000 --> 00:13:07.880
because that will be the source
of the greatest work you'll do.

00:13:07.880 --> 00:13:11.752
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:13:19.496 --> 00:13:22.900
[END PLAYBACK]

00:13:22.900 --> 00:13:24.015
MELISSA WILEY: Great.

00:13:24.015 --> 00:13:25.390
So I hope everybody
enjoyed that.

00:13:25.390 --> 00:13:26.890
My name is Melissa Wiley.

00:13:26.890 --> 00:13:29.050
And I am vice president
of digital products

00:13:29.050 --> 00:13:30.760
at National Geographic.

00:13:30.760 --> 00:13:33.190
And I'm sure as you
can probably all

00:13:33.190 --> 00:13:35.080
guess after watching
that video, I've

00:13:35.080 --> 00:13:38.210
got just about the
best job in the world.

00:13:38.210 --> 00:13:42.130
I get to work with photographers
scientists and explorers who,

00:13:42.130 --> 00:13:44.530
just like all of you
who have gathered here,

00:13:44.530 --> 00:13:46.600
care deeply about the planet.

00:13:46.600 --> 00:13:48.340
And today, I just
want to offer you

00:13:48.340 --> 00:13:53.170
a brief glimpse into how we
take our own public message

00:13:53.170 --> 00:13:55.990
about creating a
planet in balance,

00:13:55.990 --> 00:13:59.350
and we also look at
ourselves inwards

00:13:59.350 --> 00:14:02.380
to see how we can
incorporate that message

00:14:02.380 --> 00:14:04.735
into our own corporate
responsibility.

00:14:09.100 --> 00:14:13.080
So a spirit of exploration
has always really

00:14:13.080 --> 00:14:15.120
been at the heart and
soul of everything

00:14:15.120 --> 00:14:17.310
we do at National Geographic.

00:14:17.310 --> 00:14:21.900
Before there was Pinterest,
Instagram, Facebook,

00:14:21.900 --> 00:14:24.150
National Geographic
was really how

00:14:24.150 --> 00:14:27.810
we connected the world
to people, cultures,

00:14:27.810 --> 00:14:32.030
and critical changes
in the planet.

00:14:32.030 --> 00:14:35.120
But now more than ever, we
understand the importance

00:14:35.120 --> 00:14:38.270
of using photography and
storytelling helping to inspire

00:14:38.270 --> 00:14:41.920
people to take action.

00:14:41.920 --> 00:14:45.850
For us, it all starts
with a sense of purpose.

00:14:45.850 --> 00:14:49.480
National Geographic believes
that when people understand

00:14:49.480 --> 00:14:53.570
about the world around them,
they care deeply about it,

00:14:53.570 --> 00:14:56.020
and they want to take
responsibility for it.

00:14:59.520 --> 00:15:02.730
On top of our television
and our magazine,

00:15:02.730 --> 00:15:06.030
National Geographic reaches
approximately half a billion

00:15:06.030 --> 00:15:08.370
people across our
social channels.

00:15:08.370 --> 00:15:12.330
And with that scale comes
the opportunities to really

00:15:12.330 --> 00:15:14.630
bring communities
together to drive change.

00:15:17.410 --> 00:15:20.290
And that's why we
tell the stories

00:15:20.290 --> 00:15:22.450
of our photographers
and our scientists

00:15:22.450 --> 00:15:26.950
and our explorers who are
documenting our world in order

00:15:26.950 --> 00:15:29.465
to inspire everybody here
to go out and make change.

00:15:32.590 --> 00:15:34.660
But not only does
National Geographic

00:15:34.660 --> 00:15:39.410
have a long history of being a
leader at inspiring all of you,

00:15:39.410 --> 00:15:42.400
we've long been a leader
looking inward at ourselves

00:15:42.400 --> 00:15:46.210
to understand what actions we
can take towards sustainable

00:15:46.210 --> 00:15:47.780
business practices.

00:15:47.780 --> 00:15:51.970
So we've adopted a corporate
responsibility policy

00:15:51.970 --> 00:15:55.120
that we share with pretty
much everyone we work with.

00:15:55.120 --> 00:15:57.100
We share this with
employees, we share it

00:15:57.100 --> 00:16:00.350
with vendors, partners,
and all of our suppliers.

00:16:00.350 --> 00:16:04.510
So we want to work
towards carbon neutrality.

00:16:04.510 --> 00:16:08.560
We're working towards
a zero landfill waste.

00:16:08.560 --> 00:16:11.860
We want to practice wise
resource stewardship.

00:16:11.860 --> 00:16:13.360
And most importantly,
we really want

00:16:13.360 --> 00:16:15.400
to have a healthy and
engaged workforce.

00:16:18.170 --> 00:16:20.630
At Nat Geo, we're
committed to operating

00:16:20.630 --> 00:16:24.650
are the state our facilities
are sustainably as possible.

00:16:24.650 --> 00:16:26.630
Our headquarters in
Washington D.C. First

00:16:26.630 --> 00:16:29.960
became LEED certified in 2003.

00:16:29.960 --> 00:16:33.770
Through our efforts of tracking
energy, maintenance, waste,

00:16:33.770 --> 00:16:36.470
and office supplies, we
were able to increase that

00:16:36.470 --> 00:16:39.620
from silver to gold in 2009.

00:16:39.620 --> 00:16:42.860
And we actually achieved
our sixth certification

00:16:42.860 --> 00:16:46.490
just last year in 2018.

00:16:46.490 --> 00:16:49.280
In addition to this great
work, National Geographic

00:16:49.280 --> 00:16:54.060
also now receives 50%
our energy from solar.

00:16:54.060 --> 00:16:58.610
A few other accomplishments
that we achieved in just 2018,

00:16:58.610 --> 00:17:00.920
we earned Energy
Star certifications

00:17:00.920 --> 00:17:02.145
for our headquarters.

00:17:02.145 --> 00:17:03.770
We've also been going
through a process

00:17:03.770 --> 00:17:05.694
to renovate our buildings.

00:17:05.694 --> 00:17:07.069
As part of that
renovation, we're

00:17:07.069 --> 00:17:10.430
now putting a kitchen on
every floor that offers things

00:17:10.430 --> 00:17:16.069
like reusable dishware,
low landfill coffee,

00:17:16.069 --> 00:17:18.470
bottle-less water fountains.

00:17:18.470 --> 00:17:20.030
As part of our
renovations, we've

00:17:20.030 --> 00:17:23.900
been recycling tons
of office furniture,

00:17:23.900 --> 00:17:27.710
art supplies, as
well as electronics

00:17:27.710 --> 00:17:29.150
and metal from the renovations.

00:17:31.770 --> 00:17:34.320
And with that money
that we save, as well as

00:17:34.320 --> 00:17:35.940
the proceeds from
our products, we're

00:17:35.940 --> 00:17:40.950
then able to reinvest back
into our non-profit arm, who

00:17:40.950 --> 00:17:45.930
has awarded over 14,000 grants
so that more curious people

00:17:45.930 --> 00:17:48.750
can go out into the world
and inspire all of you

00:17:48.750 --> 00:17:50.010
to take action.

00:17:50.010 --> 00:17:54.120
Our goal is to identify,
cultivate, and develop

00:17:54.120 --> 00:17:56.150
the change makers of
today and tomorrow.

00:17:58.970 --> 00:18:01.760
One example of this is our
Planet or Plastic initiative

00:18:01.760 --> 00:18:04.760
that we launched in May of 2018.

00:18:04.760 --> 00:18:08.000
This is a multi-year effort
about raising awareness

00:18:08.000 --> 00:18:11.900
around the global
plastic crisis in order

00:18:11.900 --> 00:18:14.842
to help reduce the amount
of single use plastics

00:18:14.842 --> 00:18:16.550
that's making it into
the world's oceans.

00:18:21.570 --> 00:18:24.870
As part of this initiative,
not only is National Geographic

00:18:24.870 --> 00:18:26.820
asking all of you
to go out and take

00:18:26.820 --> 00:18:30.780
a look at your own usage of
single use plastic and pledge

00:18:30.780 --> 00:18:33.390
to reduce that use, we're
also taking a look inward.

00:18:33.390 --> 00:18:36.720
And we have initiated
a plastic audit

00:18:36.720 --> 00:18:39.150
across our entire
organization so

00:18:39.150 --> 00:18:41.640
that we can understand
the extent of our use

00:18:41.640 --> 00:18:45.210
across our supply chain, as well
as work towards eliminating it.

00:18:48.900 --> 00:18:50.640
So at National
Geographic, we also

00:18:50.640 --> 00:18:52.320
understand the
power of technology

00:18:52.320 --> 00:18:56.640
to create connections and drive
empathy and most importantly

00:18:56.640 --> 00:18:59.770
increase our awareness and
understanding of one another.

00:18:59.770 --> 00:19:01.570
So this is one of
my favorite videos.

00:19:01.570 --> 00:19:03.480
What you're taking
a look at here

00:19:03.480 --> 00:19:06.660
is an example of our VR
theater that we launched

00:19:06.660 --> 00:19:08.640
in October of last year.

00:19:08.640 --> 00:19:12.030
So we now have an
Oculus Go headset

00:19:12.030 --> 00:19:15.210
that is hooked up to all
400 seats in the room

00:19:15.210 --> 00:19:18.180
so that everybody
can have the same 360

00:19:18.180 --> 00:19:20.710
experience at the same time.

00:19:20.710 --> 00:19:22.620
It's really fun.

00:19:22.620 --> 00:19:25.350
However, we realized that not
only do we have the opportunity

00:19:25.350 --> 00:19:28.080
and responsibility to
connect our consumer

00:19:28.080 --> 00:19:30.072
towards environmental
issues, but we

00:19:30.072 --> 00:19:31.530
realize that we
need to take a look

00:19:31.530 --> 00:19:34.110
at our internal
infrastructure as well.

00:19:37.060 --> 00:19:40.170
So the National Geographic
Image Collection,

00:19:40.170 --> 00:19:41.850
the National Geographic
Image Collection

00:19:41.850 --> 00:19:44.850
is the record of truth
for all photography

00:19:44.850 --> 00:19:47.040
assets at National Geographic.

00:19:47.040 --> 00:19:50.280
This database not only holds
the photographs themselves,

00:19:50.280 --> 00:19:55.530
but it holds information about
contracts, rights, and actually

00:19:55.530 --> 00:19:56.650
the physical location.

00:19:56.650 --> 00:19:58.740
So if you want to go
down and actually find

00:19:58.740 --> 00:20:00.465
that original Afghan
girl print, you're

00:20:00.465 --> 00:20:02.340
going to take a look in
the Image Collection,

00:20:02.340 --> 00:20:04.757
and that's going to tell you
where to go and actually pull

00:20:04.757 --> 00:20:06.930
that print off the shelves.

00:20:06.930 --> 00:20:08.880
Last year, we found
ourselves in a situation

00:20:08.880 --> 00:20:12.270
where that system had
to be taken offline.

00:20:12.270 --> 00:20:15.450
And while that was
extremely-- and extremely

00:20:15.450 --> 00:20:16.600
is an understatement.

00:20:16.600 --> 00:20:18.420
It was extremely
inconvenient for everyone

00:20:18.420 --> 00:20:21.330
who uses the system, it
gave us an opportunity

00:20:21.330 --> 00:20:23.040
to take a step back
and really take

00:20:23.040 --> 00:20:25.320
a look at that
20-year-old system

00:20:25.320 --> 00:20:27.300
and make some critical
decisions about how we

00:20:27.300 --> 00:20:30.300
wanted to move forward with it.

00:20:30.300 --> 00:20:32.700
As a result, we decided
to partner with Google.

00:20:32.700 --> 00:20:36.420
We know that Google
shares similar passions

00:20:36.420 --> 00:20:39.390
as National Geographic
around sustainability.

00:20:39.390 --> 00:20:42.660
The actions that took this
system offline unfortunately

00:20:42.660 --> 00:20:44.580
really didn't earn
us a lot of goodwill

00:20:44.580 --> 00:20:45.990
with our photographers.

00:20:45.990 --> 00:20:48.000
And we knew that
the steps that we

00:20:48.000 --> 00:20:49.770
took to bring the
system back online,

00:20:49.770 --> 00:20:51.780
we really wanted
to be able to say

00:20:51.780 --> 00:20:54.780
that we were storing their
photographs as sustainably

00:20:54.780 --> 00:20:55.400
as possible.

00:20:55.400 --> 00:20:57.990
We wanted to be able to
prove to our photographers

00:20:57.990 --> 00:21:01.350
that we recognized the
importance of their collections

00:21:01.350 --> 00:21:04.530
and that we share
common values with them.

00:21:04.530 --> 00:21:08.220
So we partnered with Google,
and we moved this system

00:21:08.220 --> 00:21:10.330
to the cloud in just
over two months.

00:21:10.330 --> 00:21:12.180
So this was an exciting
process for us.

00:21:12.180 --> 00:21:14.367
And it's really been a
huge success story for us

00:21:14.367 --> 00:21:15.450
to bring this back online.

00:21:19.410 --> 00:21:21.840
We're now in the process
of partnering with Google

00:21:21.840 --> 00:21:24.750
to really try and understand
those energy savings that we

00:21:24.750 --> 00:21:27.540
took when we migrated this
system from our on premise

00:21:27.540 --> 00:21:29.220
facility into the cloud.

00:21:29.220 --> 00:21:31.290
So we've been able
to provide Google

00:21:31.290 --> 00:21:35.400
with a bunch of statistics
about our legacy system.

00:21:35.400 --> 00:21:37.410
And then Google is
helping us to calculate

00:21:37.410 --> 00:21:40.980
that to really understand what
savings that we've earned.

00:21:40.980 --> 00:21:44.830
So this study is still ongoing.

00:21:44.830 --> 00:21:47.000
We're just starting to get
in some early learnings.

00:21:47.000 --> 00:21:48.708
But some of the things
that we are seeing

00:21:48.708 --> 00:21:52.830
is that we are going to
incur greenhouse emissions

00:21:52.830 --> 00:21:57.090
savings over the course of a
year that equal around heating

00:21:57.090 --> 00:22:00.000
an average sized home
for about 30 to 40 months

00:22:00.000 --> 00:22:03.480
or driving a car for
about 25,000 miles.

00:22:03.480 --> 00:22:05.340
And those stats
are exciting to us.

00:22:05.340 --> 00:22:08.090
They are relatively small,
but the Image Collection, it

00:22:08.090 --> 00:22:12.455
is also a very small portion
of our overall infrastructure.

00:22:12.455 --> 00:22:14.580
And we feel like this is
going to make a great case

00:22:14.580 --> 00:22:16.950
study for us to then
go to our executives

00:22:16.950 --> 00:22:19.530
and say that we think
sustainability needs

00:22:19.530 --> 00:22:23.290
to be part of our benchmarks
in all of our infrastructure

00:22:23.290 --> 00:22:23.790
planning.

00:22:23.790 --> 00:22:25.665
It's going to help get
us towards that carbon

00:22:25.665 --> 00:22:28.810
neutral goal that we
talked about earlier.

00:22:28.810 --> 00:22:30.540
So that's just a brief look.

00:22:30.540 --> 00:22:33.930
Going to now hand
this over to Jake.

00:22:33.930 --> 00:22:35.790
I've talked to you
a lot about the work

00:22:35.790 --> 00:22:38.810
National Geographic has done
to look at our own corporation.

00:22:38.810 --> 00:22:41.340
Jake is going to come up
here and talk about products

00:22:41.340 --> 00:22:45.609
that you all can use to take
a look at your own footprint.

00:22:45.609 --> 00:22:51.760
[APPLAUSE]

00:22:51.760 --> 00:22:53.220
JAKE WACHMAN: Hi, everyone.

00:22:53.220 --> 00:22:54.140
I'm Jake Wachman.

00:22:54.140 --> 00:22:57.320
I run software product and
engineering for SunPower.

00:22:57.320 --> 00:23:00.830
And if you're not familiar with
SunPower, maybe if you are,

00:23:00.830 --> 00:23:03.200
you may know that we make
the world's most efficient

00:23:03.200 --> 00:23:04.850
solar cells and panels.

00:23:04.850 --> 00:23:07.550
And we offer those all
around the world to customers

00:23:07.550 --> 00:23:10.490
everywhere, as well as in
the US where we packaged them

00:23:10.490 --> 00:23:14.970
as solutions for residential
and commercial customers.

00:23:14.970 --> 00:23:21.260
And so it's my team's job to
make our software at SunPower

00:23:21.260 --> 00:23:23.270
as amazing as our hardware.

00:23:23.270 --> 00:23:26.390
We're here today to talk
about a new technology we've

00:23:26.390 --> 00:23:29.600
built on top of Google Cloud
that we think does that.

00:23:29.600 --> 00:23:33.000
But first, let's talk hardware.

00:23:33.000 --> 00:23:35.390
So this is the
SolarStratos plane

00:23:35.390 --> 00:23:38.690
that will soar to 80,000 feet
into the stratosphere powered

00:23:38.690 --> 00:23:42.590
just on solar
cells, specifically

00:23:42.590 --> 00:23:44.480
SunPower solar cells.

00:23:44.480 --> 00:23:45.500
Why?

00:23:45.500 --> 00:23:49.378
Because our cells are
up to 22.7% efficient.

00:23:49.378 --> 00:23:51.170
And that's exactly the
kind of power output

00:23:51.170 --> 00:23:53.337
you need when you have a
limited wingspan with which

00:23:53.337 --> 00:23:55.860
to power a plane.

00:23:55.860 --> 00:23:59.600
This plane and this team
prove that solar's here,

00:23:59.600 --> 00:24:04.370
it's ready now, it's ready for
the most difficult applications

00:24:04.370 --> 00:24:08.150
you can throw at it,
and it's powerful.

00:24:08.150 --> 00:24:10.030
It's also powerful
enough for Google,

00:24:10.030 --> 00:24:13.700
or rather Google and Alphabet's
spin-out, Project Loon.

00:24:13.700 --> 00:24:16.340
Project Loon, if
you're not aware,

00:24:16.340 --> 00:24:19.190
sends balloons aloft
for months on end

00:24:19.190 --> 00:24:22.180
to provide internet service
to remote parts of the world.

00:24:22.180 --> 00:24:24.170
And you can see down
here, it's running

00:24:24.170 --> 00:24:28.540
on SunPower solar panels
in very low temperatures

00:24:28.540 --> 00:24:32.080
all times of the day.

00:24:32.080 --> 00:24:34.130
And those are the
very same cells

00:24:34.130 --> 00:24:37.040
that go on homeowners'
roofs here in the US

00:24:37.040 --> 00:24:38.480
and around the world.

00:24:38.480 --> 00:24:39.980
You see them up
there, kind of looks

00:24:39.980 --> 00:24:45.200
like a skylight we've made
10 gigawatts of solar power

00:24:45.200 --> 00:24:47.960
since our first factory opened.

00:24:47.960 --> 00:24:53.780
That's equivalent to offsetting
40 million metric tons

00:24:53.780 --> 00:24:58.340
of carbon dioxide or roughly
taking 9 million cars

00:24:58.340 --> 00:25:00.450
off the road for a year.

00:25:00.450 --> 00:25:02.930
That's a lot of solar.

00:25:02.930 --> 00:25:07.040
But even as solar has taken
off, we've only reached about 3%

00:25:07.040 --> 00:25:08.090
of American homes.

00:25:08.090 --> 00:25:11.210
So we have 97% percent to go.

00:25:11.210 --> 00:25:14.930
And I think that we have two
big problems that we need

00:25:14.930 --> 00:25:18.380
to solve to get to that 100%.

00:25:18.380 --> 00:25:20.810
First, I'd say we have
an awareness problem.

00:25:20.810 --> 00:25:22.880
Homeowners and
businesses don't realize

00:25:22.880 --> 00:25:25.940
the extent to which solar
can save them money,

00:25:25.940 --> 00:25:29.180
can help the environment, and
also, decentralized energy

00:25:29.180 --> 00:25:31.940
can provide a degree
of energy independence.

00:25:31.940 --> 00:25:34.130
And secondly, we have
a workflow problem.

00:25:34.130 --> 00:25:37.190
Getting that solar panel on the
roof and getting it designed

00:25:37.190 --> 00:25:40.040
and getting installed is
actually a very difficult thing

00:25:40.040 --> 00:25:41.480
to solve.

00:25:41.480 --> 00:25:45.150
And so we try to solve
both with software.

00:25:45.150 --> 00:25:48.590
We want to make solar as
easy to buy and install

00:25:48.590 --> 00:25:52.190
as any other
consumer electronics.

00:25:52.190 --> 00:25:54.380
So who here remembers
televisions?

00:25:54.380 --> 00:25:56.180
Raise your hand.

00:25:56.180 --> 00:25:58.650
Yeah, not quite a relic
of the past yet, right?

00:25:58.650 --> 00:26:01.120
But buying a TV's pretty
simple and pretty easy.

00:26:01.120 --> 00:26:03.770
They come some pretty
standard sizes, right?

00:26:03.770 --> 00:26:09.170
You got 65 inches, 55 inches,
all the way down to 32.

00:26:09.170 --> 00:26:11.630
And so when you're going
through the process of buying

00:26:11.630 --> 00:26:14.180
a television as a
consumer, you think,

00:26:14.180 --> 00:26:18.680
I have a wall I have a
credenza, I have a media center,

00:26:18.680 --> 00:26:20.150
does the TV fit?

00:26:20.150 --> 00:26:21.380
Can I see it from my couch?

00:26:21.380 --> 00:26:23.900
It's pretty simple,
understanding

00:26:23.900 --> 00:26:27.260
what size of television,
how much TV you need.

00:26:27.260 --> 00:26:31.430
Well, solar is not that simple.

00:26:31.430 --> 00:26:34.810
Every house is different,
every roof unique.

00:26:34.810 --> 00:26:37.130
We're pointed in
orientation toward the sun

00:26:37.130 --> 00:26:38.352
in different ways.

00:26:38.352 --> 00:26:40.310
There are pipes and
chimneys you can't see here

00:26:40.310 --> 00:26:42.090
in this simplified diagram.

00:26:42.090 --> 00:26:44.450
And it becomes a
very complex process.

00:26:44.450 --> 00:26:47.910
It becomes a custom widget
for every homeowner.

00:26:47.910 --> 00:26:49.650
And so that's our challenge.

00:26:49.650 --> 00:26:52.250
How do we simplify that?

00:26:52.250 --> 00:26:53.930
And I'll show you
the state of the art.

00:26:53.930 --> 00:26:57.140
I'll show you how we
actually quote today.

00:26:57.140 --> 00:27:00.230
The quote is so important
to understanding the value

00:27:00.230 --> 00:27:03.013
proposition to the homeowner and
to providing them an accurate

00:27:03.013 --> 00:27:04.680
estimate of how much
energy they'll save

00:27:04.680 --> 00:27:06.980
and how much money they'll save.

00:27:06.980 --> 00:27:09.110
And so here's how
we do it today.

00:27:09.110 --> 00:27:10.320
We take a look at the roof.

00:27:10.320 --> 00:27:12.830
This is actually
deceptively complex.

00:27:12.830 --> 00:27:15.890
And we say, OK, there's
a chimney down there,

00:27:15.890 --> 00:27:19.150
there are five skylights,
can't put solar there.

00:27:19.150 --> 00:27:21.280
And I don't know if you
see this right there,

00:27:21.280 --> 00:27:22.490
but those are little pipes.

00:27:22.490 --> 00:27:23.990
They're really hard
to see, actually

00:27:23.990 --> 00:27:25.582
hard for our design team to see.

00:27:25.582 --> 00:27:27.290
They're about nine of
them, at least nine

00:27:27.290 --> 00:27:30.080
of them that I can
see with my naked eye.

00:27:30.080 --> 00:27:31.200
But that's not all.

00:27:31.200 --> 00:27:33.045
There's also local
jurisdictions.

00:27:33.045 --> 00:27:34.670
And you have to take
those into account

00:27:34.670 --> 00:27:36.990
as you're designing a
solar power system as well.

00:27:36.990 --> 00:27:39.500
And so most towns and
cities and counties

00:27:39.500 --> 00:27:42.430
require walkways
along the ridge line

00:27:42.430 --> 00:27:44.315
and along the front
and back of the house

00:27:44.315 --> 00:27:47.490
so you can actually access
the roof at any time.

00:27:47.490 --> 00:27:49.950
And so it gets
even more complex.

00:27:49.950 --> 00:27:52.760
There are trees and bushes
all around the house.

00:27:52.760 --> 00:27:54.740
And those are shading the house.

00:27:54.740 --> 00:27:56.690
So it's hard to know
where exactly should I

00:27:56.690 --> 00:27:59.450
put those solar panels and
how tall are these trees,

00:27:59.450 --> 00:28:02.210
because this isn't just a
two dimensional problem.

00:28:02.210 --> 00:28:04.160
This is a three
dimensional problem.

00:28:04.160 --> 00:28:06.870
This house actually
happens to be on a hill.

00:28:06.870 --> 00:28:09.080
And the trees, some are
higher, some are lower.

00:28:09.080 --> 00:28:12.020
You can't tell that from
the overhead view alone.

00:28:12.020 --> 00:28:16.250
And so our designers
spend 30 minutes

00:28:16.250 --> 00:28:20.480
per house thousands
of times a week

00:28:20.480 --> 00:28:22.535
to generate a solar design.

00:28:22.535 --> 00:28:26.540
And I'll show you a sped
up video of how that works,

00:28:26.540 --> 00:28:27.405
here it is.

00:28:27.405 --> 00:28:28.530
They're outlining the roof.

00:28:28.530 --> 00:28:32.235
This is the current state
of the art sped up 50x.

00:28:32.235 --> 00:28:33.860
This is what they do
2,000 times a week

00:28:33.860 --> 00:28:36.570
to generate solar designs
for our customers--

00:28:36.570 --> 00:28:39.350
making sure all the roof
planes are in the right spot,

00:28:39.350 --> 00:28:41.540
putting in those walkways
getting the roof plane

00:28:41.540 --> 00:28:47.840
just right, putting
in more walkways--

00:28:47.840 --> 00:28:50.330
so we're about five
minutes in right now--

00:28:50.330 --> 00:28:51.950
getting the pipes
and the skylights.

00:28:55.470 --> 00:29:00.840
So there are 100 million future
solar homes the United States.

00:29:00.840 --> 00:29:03.000
At our current pace,
it would take us

00:29:03.000 --> 00:29:06.300
a century to design them all.

00:29:06.300 --> 00:29:07.630
Who has time for that?

00:29:07.630 --> 00:29:10.050
I don't have time for
that, the planet certainly

00:29:10.050 --> 00:29:12.060
doesn't have time for that.

00:29:12.060 --> 00:29:15.190
So there must be a better way.

00:29:15.190 --> 00:29:19.400
And we are here today to talk
about it and announce it.

00:29:19.400 --> 00:29:22.640
We call it SunPower
Instant Design.

00:29:22.640 --> 00:29:24.640
And it's built on Google Cloud.

00:29:24.640 --> 00:29:28.520
A homeowner enters an
address and within 60 seconds

00:29:28.520 --> 00:29:30.262
gets a design back.

00:29:30.262 --> 00:29:31.970
We're using all the
best machine learning

00:29:31.970 --> 00:29:36.390
that Google Cloud has to
offer and running it on GPUs.

00:29:36.390 --> 00:29:39.143
I'll get a little bit more
into how we're doing it,

00:29:39.143 --> 00:29:40.560
but we're very
excited about this.

00:29:40.560 --> 00:29:43.760
We think this will change
how people go solar.

00:29:43.760 --> 00:29:45.140
So here's how we're doing it.

00:29:45.140 --> 00:29:50.762
We're taking SunPower's years of
design expertise, the hundreds

00:29:50.762 --> 00:29:52.220
of thousands of
solar designs we've

00:29:52.220 --> 00:29:55.130
created, along with our
world class sales platform

00:29:55.130 --> 00:29:57.620
that we use today, called EDDiE.

00:29:57.620 --> 00:30:00.140
And we're mashing it together
with what Google brings

00:30:00.140 --> 00:30:05.050
to the table, which is GCP,
which as you heard is a zero

00:30:05.050 --> 00:30:07.550
carbon emission platform-- which
is great we got zero carbon

00:30:07.550 --> 00:30:09.860
emission making more
zero carbon emissions--

00:30:09.860 --> 00:30:12.230
as well as Google
Maps and sunroof data.

00:30:12.230 --> 00:30:14.000
Google has great
imagery of the world.

00:30:14.000 --> 00:30:18.368
And we use that as our raw
data to make this all happen.

00:30:18.368 --> 00:30:19.410
Let's go a little deeper.

00:30:19.410 --> 00:30:21.530
Let's go deeper under the hood.

00:30:21.530 --> 00:30:23.450
So SunPower has those
hundreds of thousands

00:30:23.450 --> 00:30:27.380
of designs per year,
Google has the raw data.

00:30:27.380 --> 00:30:31.700
We train a model and
annotate roofs, put them

00:30:31.700 --> 00:30:36.980
in a Cloud Storage bucket, train
the model in Cloud ML Engine.

00:30:36.980 --> 00:30:40.310
And then every time a
homeowner enters an address,

00:30:40.310 --> 00:30:44.330
we run it through that
engine to detect the roof

00:30:44.330 --> 00:30:46.100
and look for the
abstractions, basically

00:30:46.100 --> 00:30:49.370
just what you saw before that
we did manually 2,000 times

00:30:49.370 --> 00:30:51.020
a week.

00:30:51.020 --> 00:30:54.020
Then once we have the
environment characterized,

00:30:54.020 --> 00:30:57.680
we lay out the solar panels,
we do a shade analysis,

00:30:57.680 --> 00:31:01.760
we create an energy estimate
and then out pops the design.

00:31:01.760 --> 00:31:06.840
That actually is a
computer generated design.

00:31:06.840 --> 00:31:09.570
So I'll show you Instant Design.

00:31:09.570 --> 00:31:11.340
Now, I have to say
we're a little scared

00:31:11.340 --> 00:31:14.250
of the curse of the live
demos, so this a video.

00:31:14.250 --> 00:31:17.250
It's actually at real
time, not sped up.

00:31:17.250 --> 00:31:20.830
But you can go check it out for
yourself, the working software,

00:31:20.830 --> 00:31:22.620
all the way over
there at the Industry

00:31:22.620 --> 00:31:26.320
Pavilion where we'll have
people to demo it all week.

00:31:26.320 --> 00:31:28.388
So here it is.

00:31:28.388 --> 00:31:29.680
And this is a test environment.

00:31:29.680 --> 00:31:31.650
This isn't really what
the homeowner would see.

00:31:31.650 --> 00:31:33.483
But we've got the
technology up and working.

00:31:33.483 --> 00:31:35.610
We're pretty
excited to share it.

00:31:35.610 --> 00:31:39.980
Homeowner enters an address,
our favorite test house,

00:31:39.980 --> 00:31:41.190
clicks Create Design.

00:31:41.190 --> 00:31:43.180
We're doing all the same
stuff you saw before.

00:31:43.180 --> 00:31:45.600
This is, however, in real
time-- outlining the roof,

00:31:45.600 --> 00:31:47.940
looking for the abstractions,
looking for the trees,

00:31:47.940 --> 00:31:50.520
laying out the panels,
figuring out the shade, which

00:31:50.520 --> 00:31:52.410
then lets us get to the energy.

00:31:52.410 --> 00:31:55.068
And then 60 seconds
later, we get a design.

00:31:55.068 --> 00:31:56.610
So it might be a
little bit of a way.

00:31:56.610 --> 00:31:58.980
Actually, it's faster than that.

00:31:58.980 --> 00:32:00.210
That's real time.

00:32:00.210 --> 00:32:03.390
That's about 17 seconds
for that particular house.

00:32:03.390 --> 00:32:04.860
And you can see it is in 3D.

00:32:04.860 --> 00:32:06.485
Well, maybe I'll show
it one more time.

00:32:11.890 --> 00:32:14.010
So it actually
takes almost as long

00:32:14.010 --> 00:32:16.830
to enter the address
as it does to run this.

00:32:16.830 --> 00:32:19.620
And we're very fortunate
to be using Platform

00:32:19.620 --> 00:32:21.750
that lets us go really fast.

00:32:21.750 --> 00:32:25.537
I've never appreciated
GPUs as much as I do today

00:32:25.537 --> 00:32:27.120
and how fast they
can actually process

00:32:27.120 --> 00:32:30.030
all this very intensive
data really quickly

00:32:30.030 --> 00:32:32.340
to get answers for
homeowners fast.

00:32:32.340 --> 00:32:34.440
So you see the 3D model there.

00:32:34.440 --> 00:32:36.987
We have all the abstractions.

00:32:36.987 --> 00:32:38.820
And then we have the
energy data, as well as

00:32:38.820 --> 00:32:40.530
much more behind that.

00:32:40.530 --> 00:32:42.330
So let me show you--

00:32:42.330 --> 00:32:46.810
these are all designs made
within Instant Design.

00:32:46.810 --> 00:32:48.630
These are all
computer generated,

00:32:48.630 --> 00:32:51.638
no human involved whatsoever.

00:32:51.638 --> 00:32:53.430
And you'll notice that
they're not perfect.

00:32:53.430 --> 00:32:55.800
A couple of panels
might be askew,

00:32:55.800 --> 00:32:58.050
or we might be missing a
spot where we could place

00:32:58.050 --> 00:33:00.420
another couple, and that's OK.

00:33:00.420 --> 00:33:02.820
As we do this more and as
we train the model more,

00:33:02.820 --> 00:33:04.920
it will only get stronger.

00:33:04.920 --> 00:33:07.110
You'll also notice, if
you're an expert in solar,

00:33:07.110 --> 00:33:09.600
that we have panels on the
north side of the roof we,

00:33:09.600 --> 00:33:12.000
have some panels
facing northeast.

00:33:12.000 --> 00:33:13.860
We've got a lot of
panels everywhere.

00:33:13.860 --> 00:33:16.410
And that's also
intentional, because we

00:33:16.410 --> 00:33:20.520
want to give
homeowners the choice

00:33:20.520 --> 00:33:23.760
to choose the aesthetic
and also system size that

00:33:23.760 --> 00:33:25.320
meets their energy needs.

00:33:25.320 --> 00:33:29.580
They get to down select from
these what we call maximum fit

00:33:29.580 --> 00:33:36.080
design, because some
homeowners may want solar

00:33:36.080 --> 00:33:39.510
on the back of their house.

00:33:39.510 --> 00:33:43.110
If it were me, I'd
want it on the front,

00:33:43.110 --> 00:33:46.110
be really proud about
it on my own house.

00:33:46.110 --> 00:33:48.990
Some homeowners
only need SunPower

00:33:48.990 --> 00:33:52.200
solar a little bit of it,
because they have a very energy

00:33:52.200 --> 00:33:55.100
efficient house.

00:33:55.100 --> 00:33:58.160
And then some homeowners
need a lot of solar,

00:33:58.160 --> 00:34:02.385
because they've got an
EV, TVs, pool, et cetera,

00:34:02.385 --> 00:34:04.760
and they need to run a lot of
energy through their house.

00:34:07.280 --> 00:34:08.540
So let's zoom up.

00:34:08.540 --> 00:34:10.159
This is why we're doing this.

00:34:10.159 --> 00:34:14.480
If Google wants a cloud computer
in every hand or on every desk,

00:34:14.480 --> 00:34:20.090
SunPower is interested
in solar on every roof.

00:34:20.090 --> 00:34:24.030
And to me,
applications like this

00:34:24.030 --> 00:34:27.655
to better the human
condition and our health

00:34:27.655 --> 00:34:29.030
and the health of
our environment

00:34:29.030 --> 00:34:32.070
are really the best uses
of machine learning.

00:34:32.070 --> 00:34:35.270
So we see SunPower
Instant Design

00:34:35.270 --> 00:34:37.580
as an early example
of everything

00:34:37.580 --> 00:34:41.600
that we can do to move the
planet and the environment

00:34:41.600 --> 00:34:43.739
forward.

00:34:43.739 --> 00:34:47.570
I also think it's one of
the finer examples of what

00:34:47.570 --> 00:34:49.760
we can do with GPUs.

00:34:49.760 --> 00:34:51.650
So imagine if we
took all those GPUs

00:34:51.650 --> 00:34:56.900
currently binding for
Bitcoin and redirected them

00:34:56.900 --> 00:34:59.443
to mine for solar in the cloud.

00:34:59.443 --> 00:35:00.860
Could we design
all those hundreds

00:35:00.860 --> 00:35:04.070
million future solar
homes in an afternoon?

00:35:04.070 --> 00:35:05.930
Potentially.

00:35:05.930 --> 00:35:09.050
So we're very excited
to launch this.

00:35:09.050 --> 00:35:11.060
It's arriving this summer.

00:35:11.060 --> 00:35:14.870
And people in select California
cities can go to our website

00:35:14.870 --> 00:35:17.330
and enter in their
address and then get

00:35:17.330 --> 00:35:20.240
a solar design for their home.

00:35:20.240 --> 00:35:23.690
Until then, go to
sunpower.com/design to learn

00:35:23.690 --> 00:35:26.850
more and to find out when
it's coming to your area.

00:35:26.850 --> 00:35:29.330
And of course, for everyone
here in the audience

00:35:29.330 --> 00:35:33.680
and at the expo, you can go
actually check it out yourself

00:35:33.680 --> 00:35:36.830
at the SunPower booth
in the energy section.

00:35:36.830 --> 00:35:39.020
Very excited to launch
this, and thank you everyone

00:35:39.020 --> 00:35:41.030
for coming to our talk.

00:35:41.030 --> 00:35:45.050
The three of us will be
around after for questions.

00:35:45.050 --> 00:35:48.590
And then I was told also
to move one forward ahead

00:35:48.590 --> 00:35:51.230
to say complete the survey.

00:35:51.230 --> 00:35:52.130
So thank you.

00:35:52.130 --> 00:35:55.430
[APPLAUSE]

00:35:55.430 --> 00:35:58.480
[MUSIC PLAYING]

